## What to produce

**`<techspec>`** — this subcomponent's design argument: what slice of
the owning component's responsibility it carries, and why the split
between this subcomponent and its siblings falls where it does. Impl
reads this to decide how to implement, so name the actual internal
shape — data owned, collaborators called, not a restatement of the
slug.

**`<privapi>`** — this subcomponent's surface toward its *siblings
inside the same component*. This is never the component's public
boundary — that's the owning comparch's `<pubapi>`, already decided and
not yours to restate or leak around. If what you're describing is
actually the thing external callers reach, you've drawn the boundary in
the wrong place; either it belongs in the parent's `<pubapi>` or it
isn't public at all.

**`<process_inventory>`** (optional) — every named process this
subcomponent registers under `processes/0`, each with a `category`
(`local | singleton | sharded`) and a `<rationale>` that argues the
choice, not just states it. This element exists specifically so
placement is designed and reviewed here, not improvised at impl time
(v5 §2.5) — "singleton, because it coordinates cluster state no node
should duplicate" is a rationale; "singleton" restated as a sentence is
not. Add `<max_heap_size>` / `<message_queue_alarm_len>` only where a
process has a real reason to need a VM-enforced ceiling — a runaway
mailbox is a plausible failure mode for this specific process, not a
box to check.

**`<store>`** (optional, but present whenever this subcomponent *is*
`Comp.Store`) — every component always has exactly one persistence
subcomponent (v5 §2.4, no size floor); if this is it, describe
`<schemas>` as the actual tables/schemas and the function-shaped
queries this store exports — never generic CRUD. "get, list, update"
is a repository description, not a store design; name the queries a
caller actually needs (`fetch_active_for_owner/1`, not `get/1`). Add
`<grade>` (`bare_log | commanded_aggregate`) only if this store belongs
to the event-sourced family (v5 §2.4) — a plain Ecto-backed store omits
it entirely.

If this subcomponent is not the store, omit `<store>` — don't describe
persistence you don't own; that's drift into a sibling's territory the
next reviewer has to untangle.
